#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba49c-4a26-54e2-bd75-42ad05f3b4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294763} {:answer/id #uuid "04abffd8-7053-53e6-b817-84082d046c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294764} {:answer/id #uuid "5a36e3d7-210d-5b4f-a85a-040387b79fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294765} {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} {:answer/id #uuid "430bfad4-077d-5435-8e5c-52a43682396e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a971d13-8c2e-5ca1-b66c-f3ad09971c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c510ba2-7634-5ceb-8cb3-a5b35044f36d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}]}
